WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.914 --> 00:00:04.743
We are not stopping
until we find that smell.

2
00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.397
Check out that black thing.

3
00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:09.226
Was that even food?

4
00:00:09.313 --> 00:00:13.230
I believe it was
cheese at one time.

5
00:00:14.797 --> 00:00:16.712
No, that's not it.

6
00:00:21.412 --> 00:00:23.240
-Ugh!
-Ew!

7
00:00:23.327 --> 00:00:25.242
-Nope.
-No.

8
00:00:25.329 --> 00:00:27.331
Hey, what about that?

9
00:00:30.247 --> 00:00:33.250
Oh, God!
Isn't that the school rat

10
00:00:33.337 --> 00:00:36.253
that Dewey was taking
care of last summer?

11
00:00:40.779 --> 00:00:43.304
-No, that's not it.
-Keep looking.

12
00:00:43.391 --> 00:00:46.829
♪ Yes, no, maybe ♪

13
00:00:46.916 --> 00:00:49.397
♪ I don't know ♪

14
00:00:49.484 --> 00:00:52.617
♪ Can you repeat
the question? ♪

15
00:00:52.704 --> 00:00:55.185
♪ You're not the boss
of me now ♪

16
00:00:55.272 --> 00:00:57.361
♪ You're not
the boss of me now ♪

17
00:00:57.448 --> 00:00:59.842
♪ You're not the boss
of me now ♪

18
00:00:59.929 --> 00:01:01.931
♪ And you're not so big ♪

19
00:01:02.018 --> 00:01:04.498
♪ You're not the boss
of me now ♪

20
00:01:04.586 --> 00:01:06.283
♪ You're not
the boss of me now ♪

21
00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:09.025
♪ You're not the boss
of me now ♪

22
00:01:09.112 --> 00:01:11.636
♪ And you're not so big ♪

23
00:01:11.723 --> 00:01:15.727
♪ Life is unfair ♪

24
00:01:19.253 --> 00:01:21.385
No, don't hang up.
We've almost made our decision.

25
00:01:21.472 --> 00:01:23.474
I get three meat toppings
for the price of one!

26
00:01:23.561 --> 00:01:25.694
No, no! Buy two pizzas,
get one free!

27
00:01:25.781 --> 00:01:26.912
That's two free cheesepizzas!

28
00:01:26.999 --> 00:01:28.523
I'd rather eat vomit!

29
00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.221
The prospect of a deal
sends this family into a frenzy.

30
00:01:31.308 --> 00:01:32.744
When you throw in pizza,

31
00:01:32.831 --> 00:01:34.746
they need me to keep
from totally losing perspective.

32
00:01:34.833 --> 00:01:35.965
Shut up, everyone!
Just shut up!

33
00:01:36.052 --> 00:01:37.793
If we're not
having deep-dish,

34
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.142
then I swear I will tear up

35
00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:41.318
these "free cheesy bread"
coupons right now!

36
00:01:41.405 --> 00:01:43.015
Damn your cheesy bread!
I need toppings!

37
00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:44.843
Why don't we just get
the stuffed crust special?

38
00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.150
And lose my free bucket of soda?
Are you insane?!

39
00:01:47.237 --> 00:01:49.065
Now, listen, Dennis,
I'm a reasonable man,

40
00:01:49.152 --> 00:01:50.849
but I can't go back to my family

41
00:01:50.936 --> 00:01:53.417
with a lot of lawyer talk
about coupon expiration dates.

42
00:01:53.504 --> 00:01:55.593
So why don't we just reduce
the two three-fers to two-fers?

43
00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.291
Five toppings on each,

44
00:01:57.378 --> 00:01:58.988
and we'll just grandfather in
the wacky wings, okay?

45
00:01:59.075 --> 00:02:00.642
No, no, hold on, hold on.

46
00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:02.252
This call-waiting guy
will nottake a hint!

47
00:02:02.339 --> 00:02:04.167
Hello. I can't talk right now.
I...

48
00:02:04.254 --> 00:02:06.822
Yes.

49
00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:08.389
What?

50
00:02:08.476 --> 00:02:11.348
Dad, come on, you're
gonna lose those wings!

51
00:02:13.263 --> 00:02:15.396
What?

52
00:02:15.483 --> 00:02:18.312
My father died.

53
00:02:22.577 --> 00:02:24.405
It was weird
going to Grandpa's funeral.

54
00:02:24.492 --> 00:02:26.494
We hardly even knew
we had a grandpa,

55
00:02:26.581 --> 00:02:28.104
and now we don't.

56
00:02:28.191 --> 00:02:30.237
Reese is taking it really hard.

57
00:02:30.324 --> 00:02:31.977
:
Why! Why!

58
00:02:32.064 --> 00:02:35.416
Why couldn't he die
beforehe lost all his money?

59
00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:40.769
You okay?

60
00:02:40.856 --> 00:02:43.206
Yeah, I'm fine.

61
00:02:43.293 --> 00:02:44.338
Are you sure?

62
00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.820
It seems a little weird that
the second you come home

63
00:02:48.907 --> 00:02:52.084
from your father's funeral,
you turn on Sports Bloopers.

64
00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:53.477
-Hey.
-Hal...

65
00:02:53.564 --> 00:02:56.306
it is not healthy to keep
all this bottled up.

66
00:02:56.393 --> 00:02:59.962
I agree. I just don't have
anything to un-bottle.

67
00:03:00.049 --> 00:03:03.313
I mean, I thought I'd cry
when I saw him in the box.

68
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.272
I thought I'd cry
when they closed it.

69
00:03:05.359 --> 00:03:07.883
I thought I'd cry
when they put it in the ground

70
00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:11.408
and I threw dirt on him,
but... nothing.

71
00:03:11.495 --> 00:03:13.149
Actually, I did get
a little misty

72
00:03:13.236 --> 00:03:15.543
listening to the funeral
we passed on the way out.

73
00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.762
Do you know that man
was the shoeshine guy

74
00:03:17.849 --> 00:03:20.156
on the same corner for 40 years?

75
00:03:20.243 --> 00:03:23.333
Hal, you don't have to be brave.

76
00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:25.640
I'm not. You know me.

77
00:03:25.727 --> 00:03:28.251
I cried at that movie
about the boxing wallaby.

78
00:03:28.338 --> 00:03:29.339
I remember.

79
00:03:29.426 --> 00:03:31.298
I think I'm not feeling anything

80
00:03:31.385 --> 00:03:34.170
because I never really had
a relationship with him.

81
00:03:34.257 --> 00:03:35.563
I mean, I suppose
he knew my name,

82
00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.565
but he'd always just
called me "buddy."

83
00:03:37.652 --> 00:03:38.696
Oh, Hal.

84
00:03:38.783 --> 00:03:40.742
So, now a relationship that was

85
00:03:40.829 --> 00:03:44.398
totally meaningless and trivial
is over.

86
00:03:44.485 --> 00:03:47.183
It's hard to get too upset.

87
00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:53.015
:
Hey, Lois. I'm returning

88
00:03:53.102 --> 00:03:55.452
the Newsweekyou lent me
on Y2K.

89
00:03:55.539 --> 00:03:57.149
It's okay, Abe.
Hal's asleep.

90
00:03:57.237 --> 00:03:59.326
Good.
How's he doing?

91
00:03:59.413 --> 00:04:01.545
Well, he seems to be doing fine,
but he can't be.

92
00:04:01.632 --> 00:04:03.155
His father just died.

93
00:04:03.243 --> 00:04:05.201
He got more upset when
his transmission gave out.

94
00:04:05.288 --> 00:04:07.377
Well, he did just
have it rebuilt.

95
00:04:07.464 --> 00:04:08.813
But I know what you're saying.

96
00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.033
Sounds like he needs
to talk to someone.

97
00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:13.731
Abe, thank you!
I know he respects you.

98
00:04:13.818 --> 00:04:15.994
Not me.I don't want
to see him cry.

99
00:04:16.081 --> 00:04:17.779
He needs to talk
to a professional.

100
00:04:17.866 --> 00:04:18.867
A therapist?

101
00:04:18.954 --> 00:04:20.390
No, a professional actor.

102
00:04:20.476 --> 00:04:24.438
I suggest William Shatner,
TV's Captain Kirk.

103
00:04:24.525 --> 00:04:25.830
What?

104
00:04:25.917 --> 00:04:27.267
That's who I turn to
in a time of need.

105
00:04:27.354 --> 00:04:28.355
Lois...

106
00:04:28.442 --> 00:04:30.748
there's this wonderful service

107
00:04:30.835 --> 00:04:33.838
where they hook up ordinary
people with celebrities.

108
00:04:33.925 --> 00:04:35.318
You pay a fee,

109
00:04:35.405 --> 00:04:37.755
and you get a phone
call from your choice

110
00:04:37.842 --> 00:04:39.409
of participating
TV or movie stars.

111
00:04:39.496 --> 00:04:41.933
Well, mostly TV stars.

112
00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.762
And you think this is something
Hal would want?

113
00:04:44.849 --> 00:04:48.549
Absolutely! He's a huge
fan of classic Trek.

114
00:04:48.636 --> 00:04:50.855
It's not something you
share with your wife.

115
00:04:50.942 --> 00:04:52.161
Lois, believe me,

116
00:04:52.248 --> 00:04:53.597
I don't think I
would've gotten

117
00:04:53.684 --> 00:04:55.338
through my social
anxiety disorder

118
00:04:55.425 --> 00:04:58.472
without a few late-night
discussions with Agent Mulder.

119
00:04:58.559 --> 00:05:03.172
It turns out the truth
was right in here.

120
00:05:03.259 --> 00:05:06.001
Apparently, Shatner's
not available.

121
00:05:06.088 --> 00:05:07.394
He's being knighted.

122
00:05:07.481 --> 00:05:08.960
Well, thanks for trying, Abe.

123
00:05:09.047 --> 00:05:10.527
Oh, it's not over yet.

124
00:05:10.614 --> 00:05:13.661
I am not going to rest
until a classic TV star

125
00:05:13.748 --> 00:05:16.316
puts Hal's spirit at ease.

126
00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:37.337
:
I know you didn't love him,
but this is a funeral.

127
00:05:37.424 --> 00:05:38.773
Don't you care?

128
00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:40.688
You kidding?
About thisguy?

129
00:05:40.775 --> 00:05:42.342
We weren't even close.

130
00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:47.477
Check it out.

131
00:05:47.564 --> 00:05:49.044
:
Now I'm happy.

132
00:05:49.131 --> 00:05:51.263
:
Now I'm sad.

133
00:05:51.351 --> 00:05:52.874
:
Oh, now I'm happy.

134
00:05:52.961 --> 00:05:55.224
:
Boo-hoo, now I'm sad...

135
00:05:56.834 --> 00:05:59.533
Hey, dead guy, why are
you hitting yourself?

136
00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:01.186
Who knew we could have fun
with Dad?

137
00:06:01.273 --> 00:06:03.145
That's who that is?

138
00:06:03.232 --> 00:06:05.147
I thought it was, like,
a math teacher or something.

139
00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:06.801
Why aren't you sad?

140
00:06:06.888 --> 00:06:09.064
Why aren't you throwing
yourselves on my coffin

141
00:06:09.151 --> 00:06:11.806
and begging to be buried
instead of me?

142
00:06:11.893 --> 00:06:14.809
Are you guys feeling
as little as I am?

143
00:06:14.896 --> 00:06:17.028
I felt sadder
when my wart fell off.

144
00:06:17.115 --> 00:06:19.248
I guess he just didn't make
much of an impression on us

145
00:06:19.335 --> 00:06:20.467
when he was alive.

146
00:06:22.033 --> 00:06:23.687
HAL:
Boys...

147
00:06:23.774 --> 00:06:25.689
boys, don't go.

148
00:06:27.256 --> 00:06:28.475
Oh...

149
00:06:28.562 --> 00:06:29.737
Dewey,

150
00:06:29.824 --> 00:06:31.695
you always loved me.

151
00:06:37.701 --> 00:06:39.050
No, boys!

152
00:06:39.137 --> 00:06:40.835
Don't you miss me?

153
00:06:40.922 --> 00:06:43.054
Imiss me.

154
00:06:57.982 --> 00:07:00.985
Wake up, boys! You don't want
breakfast to melt!

155
00:07:01.072 --> 00:07:02.900
-Ice cream?
-Well, last time I checked,

156
00:07:02.987 --> 00:07:05.860
you can't have a sundae bar
without it.

157
00:07:05.947 --> 00:07:08.515
HAL :
Wake up, Malcolm.

158
00:07:08.602 --> 00:07:09.341
Aah!

159
00:07:11.518 --> 00:07:14.085
Dad... it's 5:00 in the morning.

160
00:07:14.172 --> 00:07:16.174
Well, I couldn't sleep
until my sons are as happy

161
00:07:16.261 --> 00:07:17.306
as I can make them.

162
00:07:17.393 --> 00:07:18.394
Today's special,

163
00:07:18.481 --> 00:07:20.570
every scoop comes
with a hug!

164
00:07:20.657 --> 00:07:22.485
And it doesn't stop here.

165
00:07:22.572 --> 00:07:24.052
We've got a full day

166
00:07:24.139 --> 00:07:25.575
of pick-up basketball
in the park,

167
00:07:25.662 --> 00:07:27.490
a barbecue, more ice cream,

168
00:07:27.577 --> 00:07:29.797
then I thought we'd take a nap
underneath a tree,

169
00:07:29.884 --> 00:07:31.015
and then catch fireflies.

170
00:07:31.102 --> 00:07:31.886
What about school?

171
00:07:31.973 --> 00:07:33.365
I've got that covered.

172
00:07:33.453 --> 00:07:35.803
Please excuse my son.
He has... the flu...

173
00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:37.326
explosive diarrhea...

174
00:07:37.413 --> 00:07:38.632
a rare bone disease...

175
00:07:38.719 --> 00:07:40.111
We may have to get you a cane.

176
00:07:40.198 --> 00:07:42.070
-Cool!
-Does Mom know?

177
00:07:42.157 --> 00:07:44.812
Please, will you let me worry
about your mom, okay?

178
00:07:44.899 --> 00:07:46.248
Seriously, though,
don't tell her.

179
00:07:46.335 --> 00:07:48.903
Now, who wants a ride
on top of the car?

180
00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:51.296
Abe, I appreciate what you're
trying to do, but I'm not...

181
00:07:51.383 --> 00:07:52.994
Lois, do you know
what it cost me to get

182
00:07:53.081 --> 00:07:54.561
Leonard Nimoy's private number?

183
00:07:54.648 --> 00:07:56.780
Ferengi noses
don't grow on trees.

184
00:07:56.867 --> 00:07:59.217
-Let's just...
-It's ringing!

185
00:07:59.304 --> 00:08:01.568
: Hello?
Mr. Nimoy?

186
00:08:01.655 --> 00:08:02.482
This is he.

187
00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:07.051
We apologize for disturbing
an important man like yourself.

188
00:08:07.138 --> 00:08:09.227
But my friend's father
just died,

189
00:08:09.314 --> 00:08:12.100
and he could use some sage
advice from a learned actor-

190
00:08:12.187 --> 00:08:15.190
director-poet-
photographer-vocalist.

191
00:08:15.277 --> 00:08:19.107
Uh, I'm sorry,
but in Star Trek II:
The Wrath of Khan,

192
00:08:19.194 --> 00:08:22.197
was Ricardo Montalban
the gentlemen he seems to be?

193
00:08:22.284 --> 00:08:24.112
Because a little birdy
told me...

194
00:08:24.199 --> 00:08:25.722
-Abe!
-NIMOY: I'd be happy
to help your friend.

195
00:08:25.809 --> 00:08:28.856
It's very sad
when a man loses his father.

196
00:08:28.943 --> 00:08:30.466
I've been through loss myself.

197
00:08:30.553 --> 00:08:33.991
Oh, that's very kind
of you, Mr. Nimoy.

198
00:08:34.078 --> 00:08:35.775
I'm his wife.

199
00:08:35.863 --> 00:08:37.952
So will this be charged
to your credit card?

200
00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:39.823
What?

201
00:08:39.909 --> 00:08:41.695
Well, that's eight dollars
a minute for the Nimoy.

202
00:08:41.782 --> 00:08:43.434
And for another five,

203
00:08:43.523 --> 00:08:45.829
I'll leave an outgoing message on your answering machine as...

204
00:08:45.916 --> 00:08:48.049
:
Mr. Bob Hope!
How about that Brooke Shields?

205
00:08:48.136 --> 00:08:50.399
She's got legs that go all
the way down to the ground...

206
00:08:50.486 --> 00:08:53.097
Wow!
Nimoy's Bob Hope is great.

207
00:08:53.184 --> 00:08:55.491
Is there nothing
the man can't do?

208
00:08:58.886 --> 00:09:00.191
Hey.

209
00:09:00.278 --> 00:09:01.976
You doing okay?

210
00:09:02.063 --> 00:09:04.456
Oh, yeah, you know, I'll drop
the kids off at school,

211
00:09:04.544 --> 00:09:06.154
maybe look through
a photo album.

212
00:09:06.241 --> 00:09:08.809
Well, that's what
bereavement days are for.

213
00:09:08.896 --> 00:09:12.290
I love you.
I love you, too.

214
00:09:19.994 --> 00:09:21.212
:
Okay!

215
00:09:21.299 --> 00:09:23.345
Yeah!
Yeah!

216
00:09:23.432 --> 00:09:25.869
Clear shot to the refrigerator.
Who's gonna stop me?

217
00:09:25.956 --> 00:09:28.611
Whoo!
Yeah!

218
00:09:28.698 --> 00:09:30.004
Nice!

219
00:10:05.866 --> 00:10:06.736
Mmm.

220
00:10:06.823 --> 00:10:07.737
Pudding!

221
00:10:19.662 --> 00:10:21.838
:
It's your mother!

222
00:10:21.925 --> 00:10:23.710
MALCOLM: Hurry!

223
00:10:27.583 --> 00:10:29.454
Oh, hi, honey.

224
00:10:30.586 --> 00:10:32.980
God, I hate school.

225
00:10:33.067 --> 00:10:35.199
What's to eat
in this dump?

226
00:10:36.505 --> 00:10:38.768
-Good night, boys.
-Good night, Dad.

227
00:10:38.855 --> 00:10:40.770
What a day.
Memorable, huh?

228
00:10:40.857 --> 00:10:41.989
And if you thought

229
00:10:42.076 --> 00:10:43.294
today was fun,

230
00:10:43.381 --> 00:10:45.035
tomorrow's gonna make it
look like garbage.

231
00:10:46.167 --> 00:10:47.603
Quick question.

232
00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:49.736
If I died in my sleep tonight,
who'd be sad?

233
00:10:49.823 --> 00:10:51.389
I would!
Me! Me!

234
00:10:52.869 --> 00:10:54.958
Sure. Of course
we'd be sad.

235
00:10:55.045 --> 00:10:56.699
You boys have just earned
some s'mores.

236
00:10:56.786 --> 00:10:57.700
Back in a jiff.

237
00:10:58.832 --> 00:10:59.876
Wait, Dad.

238
00:10:59.963 --> 00:11:02.662
You don't have
to do this.

239
00:11:02.749 --> 00:11:04.402
I think I know
what's going on.

240
00:11:04.489 --> 00:11:05.839
If you want to
talk about stuff...

241
00:11:05.926 --> 00:11:07.928
Malcolm, all I'm interested in
right now

242
00:11:08.015 --> 00:11:11.148
is filling your bellies
with marshmallow-y goodness.

243
00:11:11.235 --> 00:11:12.715
-But, Dad...
-Don't worry.

244
00:11:12.802 --> 00:11:14.499
I'll make yours double thick.

245
00:11:15.587 --> 00:11:17.459
This is so awesome.

246
00:11:17.546 --> 00:11:19.548
Yeah. I don't know what alien
worm burrowed into Dad's brain,

247
00:11:19.635 --> 00:11:21.071
but I'd like to shake its hand.

248
00:11:21.158 --> 00:11:22.638
Don't you guys see
what's going on?

249
00:11:22.725 --> 00:11:24.422
This is all because of Grandpa.

250
00:11:24.509 --> 00:11:25.902
Ever since he died,
Dad's been feeling bad

251
00:11:25.989 --> 00:11:27.425
because he doesn't
miss him enough.

252
00:11:27.512 --> 00:11:29.558
So now he's trying to compensate
by bonding with us.

253
00:11:29.645 --> 00:11:30.864
That's just a lot
of fancy language

254
00:11:30.951 --> 00:11:32.039
for all-you-can-eat s'mores.

255
00:11:32.126 --> 00:11:33.997
This isn't fun and games, Reese.

256
00:11:34.084 --> 00:11:36.391
He's got a real
emotional problem.

257
00:11:38.088 --> 00:11:41.265
You think Dad could really be
doing this to buy our love?

258
00:11:41.352 --> 00:11:42.789
I guess.

259
00:11:42.876 --> 00:11:46.444
I say we squeeze him
for all he's got.

260
00:11:46.531 --> 00:11:48.403
It's the least we
could do for him.

261
00:11:56.628 --> 00:11:57.629
Where's Dad?

262
00:11:57.717 --> 00:11:58.761
It was weird.

263
00:11:58.848 --> 00:12:00.241
I got up early
this morning

264
00:12:00.328 --> 00:12:01.633
and found him sitting
at the kitchen table

265
00:12:01.721 --> 00:12:03.200
looking at a picture
of him and Grandpa

266
00:12:03.287 --> 00:12:04.506
with this peaceful
smile on his face.

267
00:12:04.593 --> 00:12:05.768
-Really?
-Yeah.

268
00:12:05.855 --> 00:12:06.900
Then he said he
really worked

269
00:12:06.987 --> 00:12:08.205
some things out
last night.

270
00:12:08.292 --> 00:12:09.076
And then he just
took off for work.

271
00:12:09.163 --> 00:12:10.512
Wow.

272
00:12:10.599 --> 00:12:12.470
Hey, get your greedy
mitts off my lunch!

273
00:12:12.557 --> 00:12:14.124
I was just moving it
to get to my lunch.

274
00:12:14.211 --> 00:12:15.952
What lunch?

275
00:12:16.953 --> 00:12:18.520
Later.

276
00:12:19.651 --> 00:12:20.652
Get off me!

277
00:12:25.701 --> 00:12:26.833
Found you!

278
00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:27.877
Hey!

279
00:12:27.964 --> 00:12:29.357
Well, you have to admit

280
00:12:29.444 --> 00:12:31.011
it was pretty good
hiding place.

281
00:12:31.098 --> 00:12:33.187
-I was in there for over
an hour!
-I helped.

282
00:12:33.274 --> 00:12:34.928
And you should
share the prize.

283
00:12:35.015 --> 00:12:36.320
Hey, where's Malcolm?

284
00:12:36.407 --> 00:12:37.669
He had to go to school.

285
00:12:37.757 --> 00:12:39.193
Yeah, he said this was stupid.

286
00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:42.500
Too bad. I guess
he misses out on Motocross!

287
00:12:42.587 --> 00:12:44.111
Sweet!

288
00:12:44.198 --> 00:12:47.767
I mean, I guess it's okay.

289
00:12:47.854 --> 00:12:50.117
Come on. It'll be
better than yesterday.

290
00:12:50.204 --> 00:12:51.553
What was yesterday?

291
00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:53.598
I can't quite remember.
Can you, Dewey?

292
00:12:53.685 --> 00:12:55.513
I knocked you
into pudding.

293
00:12:57.298 --> 00:12:59.343
I guess the problem with
memories is they fade.

294
00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:01.041
Especially in the minds
of young people

295
00:13:01.128 --> 00:13:03.391
that are scrambled with
video games and death metal.

296
00:13:03.478 --> 00:13:04.653
I can barely remember
my own name.

297
00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:06.786
Hey, you know
what doesn't fade?

298
00:13:06.873 --> 00:13:08.048
Things.

299
00:13:08.135 --> 00:13:09.571
Like these pants.

300
00:13:09.658 --> 00:13:11.660
I can still remember
the crisp September day

301
00:13:11.747 --> 00:13:13.270
when Mom gave them to us.

302
00:13:13.357 --> 00:13:15.098
She had a little lipstick
on her upper tooth.

303
00:13:15.185 --> 00:13:16.708
Her eyes crinkled...

304
00:13:16.796 --> 00:13:18.232
She smelled like lilacs.

305
00:13:18.319 --> 00:13:19.450
Hey, you know what?

306
00:13:19.537 --> 00:13:21.061
Ican get you things.

307
00:13:21.148 --> 00:13:22.889
I don't know.

308
00:13:22.976 --> 00:13:24.629
You'd have to drive us to
some kind of mall or something.

309
00:13:24.716 --> 00:13:26.196
You boys go get in the car.

310
00:13:26.283 --> 00:13:27.719
I'm going to call
the credit card company

311
00:13:27.807 --> 00:13:29.025
and have them up my limit.

312
00:13:29.112 --> 00:13:31.549
Taken care of.

313
00:13:33.290 --> 00:13:35.075
Nice.

314
00:13:35.162 --> 00:13:38.382
It's just a shame I'll have
to catch it with a dirty mitt.

315
00:13:42.473 --> 00:13:44.127
I want to remember

316
00:13:44.214 --> 00:13:45.433
how great you are in spring,

317
00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:46.869
summer, fall...

318
00:13:46.956 --> 00:13:48.871
and winter, too.

319
00:13:50.003 --> 00:13:52.744
Wow, Dad, you bought
the whole store!

320
00:13:52.832 --> 00:13:55.530
Nobody's got
a better dad than us.

321
00:13:55.617 --> 00:13:57.575
Except Joe Garoogian.

322
00:13:57.662 --> 00:13:58.663
Dewey!

323
00:13:58.750 --> 00:13:59.664
You don't deserve
to remember

324
00:13:59.751 --> 00:14:01.014
how good Dad is.

325
00:14:01.101 --> 00:14:02.363
You only deserve to remember me

326
00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:03.538
whacking the crap out of you.

327
00:14:03.625 --> 00:14:04.974
Not so fast, Reese.

328
00:14:05.061 --> 00:14:07.455
The boy's just being honest.
Now tell me,

329
00:14:07.542 --> 00:14:10.632
what is it about this Joe's
father that's so great?

330
00:14:10.719 --> 00:14:13.156
His dad's vice president
of an electronics company.

331
00:14:13.243 --> 00:14:16.768
He can get any type of TV,
video game or stereo he wants.

332
00:14:16.856 --> 00:14:18.814
Is there an electronics store
near here?

333
00:14:18.901 --> 00:14:19.771
Tech Boys on Willow.

334
00:14:19.859 --> 00:14:21.034
Price Town's closer,

335
00:14:21.121 --> 00:14:22.862
but they have crap
for high-def.

336
00:14:29.216 --> 00:14:30.608
I've got something for Hal

337
00:14:30.695 --> 00:14:32.393
that I think may
do the trick.

338
00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:34.743
Abe, I thought
we were done with this.

339
00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:36.571
I thought we were, too, Lois,

340
00:14:36.658 --> 00:14:38.225
until 3:00 this morning

341
00:14:38.312 --> 00:14:40.705
when I found myself in a
collectibles auction site

342
00:14:40.792 --> 00:14:42.272
and saw this baby.

343
00:14:45.841 --> 00:14:47.582
Yes, it's a Vulcan lyre

344
00:14:47.669 --> 00:14:49.932
with ten preprogrammed
Christmas carols.

345
00:14:52.935 --> 00:14:55.111
Abe, I'm sorry.
I know you're trying to help,

346
00:14:55.198 --> 00:14:57.766
but this is not what Hal needs.

347
00:14:57.853 --> 00:14:59.463
You're right.

348
00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:01.726
I guess I got carried
away and lost sight

349
00:15:01.813 --> 00:15:03.728
of what got us started
in the first place...

350
00:15:03.815 --> 00:15:07.080
to hook Hal up with a member of
the original cast of Star Trek.

351
00:15:07.167 --> 00:15:08.342
That's not what got
this started.

352
00:15:08.429 --> 00:15:10.344
Abe, Hal's father died.

353
00:15:10.431 --> 00:15:12.563
And I will not let him
die in vain.

354
00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:13.869
I will call Sulu.

355
00:15:13.956 --> 00:15:15.479
I will call Chekhov.

356
00:15:15.566 --> 00:15:16.916
And if they say no,

357
00:15:17.003 --> 00:15:20.049
so help me,
I will call the Gorn.

358
00:15:20.136 --> 00:15:22.138
Abe, stop, just stop.

359
00:15:22.225 --> 00:15:24.140
I appreciate
you trying to help,

360
00:15:24.227 --> 00:15:25.663
but I don't need
any of this.

361
00:15:25.750 --> 00:15:27.709
I'm going to take Hal
to a bed and breakfast

362
00:15:27.796 --> 00:15:29.189
for a nice weekend,
and that's it.

363
00:15:29.276 --> 00:15:30.668
He just needs
some emotional comfort.

364
00:15:30.755 --> 00:15:33.149
So you think a weekend
of nurturing and sex

365
00:15:33.236 --> 00:15:34.542
is going to get him
through this?

366
00:15:34.629 --> 00:15:36.979
Do you even know this man?!

367
00:15:40.069 --> 00:15:41.592
What the hell's all this?

368
00:15:41.679 --> 00:15:44.117
Turns out Dad still has
some grieving left to do.

369
00:15:44.204 --> 00:15:45.509
I can't believe you.

370
00:15:45.596 --> 00:15:46.902
You lied to me

371
00:15:46.989 --> 00:15:47.816
and you totally
took advantage of him.

372
00:15:47.903 --> 00:15:48.904
I don't see it that way.

373
00:15:48.991 --> 00:15:50.819
They say money
can't buy love...

374
00:15:50.906 --> 00:15:52.125
but they're wrong.

375
00:15:52.212 --> 00:15:54.214
I'm actually
starting to love Dad.

376
00:15:54.301 --> 00:15:56.303
Now I'm not saying
somebody couldn't come
along and outbid him,

377
00:15:56.390 --> 00:15:58.827
but right now,
he's the front-runner.

378
00:15:58.914 --> 00:16:00.220
You can't do this.

379
00:16:00.307 --> 00:16:01.699
This is wrong.

380
00:16:01.786 --> 00:16:02.874
You're giving all this back.

381
00:16:02.962 --> 00:16:04.746
Who are you
to question his process?

382
00:16:04.833 --> 00:16:05.790
Reese, he's crazy.

383
00:16:05.877 --> 00:16:06.922
Mom's crazy.

384
00:16:07.009 --> 00:16:08.271
But she's the kind of crazy

385
00:16:08.358 --> 00:16:10.317
where all she does
is yell at us and punish.

386
00:16:10.404 --> 00:16:13.711
Now Dad's crazy in a way
where he buys us an Xbox 360.

387
00:16:13.798 --> 00:16:15.148
And you want to mess with that?

388
00:16:15.235 --> 00:16:17.150
You're just afraid
of happiness, Malcolm.

389
00:16:17.237 --> 00:16:18.673
You can delude yourself
all you want.

390
00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:20.196
All this is going back
to the store.

391
00:16:20.283 --> 00:16:22.068
Hey, give that back!
You had your chance!

392
00:16:22.155 --> 00:16:24.200
Reese, this is over!
This is the only good thing

393
00:16:24.287 --> 00:16:26.507
that's ever happened around here
and you're ruining it!

394
00:16:26.594 --> 00:16:27.682
Boys! What are you doing?

395
00:16:27.769 --> 00:16:29.466
Today's a happy day.

396
00:16:29.553 --> 00:16:31.642
You're supposed to remember
this the rest of your lives.

397
00:16:31.729 --> 00:16:33.079
I need to talk to you, Dad.

398
00:16:33.166 --> 00:16:34.558
Dad, if you love us,
don't listen to him.

399
00:16:34.645 --> 00:16:36.169
Look at what you're doing.

400
00:16:36.256 --> 00:16:37.648
Look at the money
you're spending.

401
00:16:37.735 --> 00:16:39.302
Look at the reason
why you're spending it.

402
00:16:39.389 --> 00:16:40.869
Something is very wrong.

403
00:16:40.956 --> 00:16:44.003
I hear you, Malcolm.

404
00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:48.529
I understand that things have
gotten a little unbalanced

405
00:16:48.616 --> 00:16:50.574
and I know what
I need to do.

406
00:16:50.661 --> 00:16:52.663
Thank God.

407
00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:54.622
I'm going to buy you a car.

408
00:16:57.668 --> 00:17:00.367
It's moments like this
that really define character.

409
00:17:00.454 --> 00:17:02.978
I love you, Daddy!

410
00:17:05.675 --> 00:17:06.938
No, it's not our honeymoon,

411
00:17:07.026 --> 00:17:09.550
but I would like
the honeymoon suite.

412
00:17:09.637 --> 00:17:11.117
What?

413
00:17:11.204 --> 00:17:13.336
No, that must be a mistake.

414
00:17:13.423 --> 00:17:14.945
My credit card
can't be maxed out.

415
00:17:15.034 --> 00:17:16.209
I just paid the bill.

416
00:17:18.079 --> 00:17:19.994
Reese!

417
00:17:20.082 --> 00:17:21.561
Get me out of this.

418
00:17:29.744 --> 00:17:31.702
Malcolm's getting a car.

419
00:17:31.789 --> 00:17:33.574
We only did this
so Dad wouldn't buy us

420
00:17:33.661 --> 00:17:34.618
something more expensive.

421
00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:38.361
I miss Grandpa.

422
00:17:43.105 --> 00:17:44.715
I know this is wrong.

423
00:17:44.802 --> 00:17:47.892
But somehow my body's not doing
anything to stop it.

424
00:17:47.979 --> 00:17:50.765
Maybe that means at some level,
I don't even know it's wrong.

425
00:17:50.852 --> 00:17:52.593
And if I don't know
if it's wrong,

426
00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:54.377
then I can't get blamed
for doing it.

427
00:17:54.464 --> 00:17:55.900
Right?

428
00:17:55.987 --> 00:17:57.206
Now, the high-tech package

429
00:17:57.293 --> 00:17:59.600
comes with a CG
rearview imaging system,

430
00:17:59.687 --> 00:18:02.211
a GPS and a DVD.

431
00:18:02.298 --> 00:18:04.735
I don't absolutely need
a DVD player.

432
00:18:04.822 --> 00:18:06.694
Sure you do, Malcolm.

433
00:18:06.781 --> 00:18:08.522
Every time you stop
at a red light,

434
00:18:08.609 --> 00:18:11.351
I want you watching movies,
thinking of your dad.

435
00:18:11.438 --> 00:18:13.309
-What else have we got?
-Want the sports package?

436
00:18:13.396 --> 00:18:16.225
That's like asking
if we want doors.

437
00:18:17.183 --> 00:18:20.316
There it is,
every option we offer,

438
00:18:20.403 --> 00:18:21.883
including some
I didn't even know about.

439
00:18:21.970 --> 00:18:24.190
Floor mats have
undercoating.

440
00:18:24.277 --> 00:18:26.496
Comes with
a 14-year warranty.

441
00:18:26.583 --> 00:18:27.889
We don't sell
many of those.

442
00:18:27.976 --> 00:18:29.282
All it needs...

443
00:18:29.369 --> 00:18:31.371
is your signature.

444
00:18:38.726 --> 00:18:40.249
Huh...

445
00:18:40.336 --> 00:18:41.903
What?

446
00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:43.644
My dad had this same pen.

447
00:18:43.731 --> 00:18:45.820
He must've been a great guy.
This is a great pen.

448
00:18:45.907 --> 00:18:47.909
We don't really need
to get into that right now.

449
00:18:47.996 --> 00:18:49.345
See, I just lost my dad.

450
00:18:49.432 --> 00:18:51.434
I'm sorry.

451
00:18:51.521 --> 00:18:54.350
Bet he would've loved
to have seen his grandson

452
00:18:54.437 --> 00:18:56.918
driving that sports pack.

453
00:18:57.005 --> 00:18:59.573
You know...

454
00:18:59.660 --> 00:19:01.096
I don't know
if he would have.

455
00:19:01.183 --> 00:19:04.317
He was pretty
emotionally distant.

456
00:19:04.404 --> 00:19:06.710
He had a lot of trouble
showing love.

457
00:19:06.797 --> 00:19:09.713
Yeah.

458
00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:12.803
Old-timers, eh?

459
00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:15.806
But I didn't care.

460
00:19:15.893 --> 00:19:18.244
I didn't care
if he had trouble.

461
00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:24.337
I loved him.

462
00:19:24.424 --> 00:19:26.382
He was my dad.

463
00:19:26.469 --> 00:19:29.211
You know, I see that
you're in a lot of pain

464
00:19:29.298 --> 00:19:31.082
right now...

465
00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:33.084
I'm going to need some
grief counseling, too,

466
00:19:33.172 --> 00:19:37.045
if I let you take me up on
this offer I'm giving you.

467
00:19:37.132 --> 00:19:40.440
I remember coming downstairs
in the morning

468
00:19:40.527 --> 00:19:43.225
and he would be at the table
and he would say, "Hey, buddy,

469
00:19:43.312 --> 00:19:45.009
"I'm doing four things at once:

470
00:19:45.096 --> 00:19:48.404
"I'm watching TV,
I'm reading the newspaper,

471
00:19:48.491 --> 00:19:50.363
I'm drinking coffee,
and I'm talking to you."

472
00:19:51.799 --> 00:19:54.671
Isn't that amazing?

473
00:19:54.758 --> 00:19:56.978
You know, I got a guy
whose wife died

474
00:19:57.065 --> 00:19:58.588
who's looking at
this car, so...

475
00:19:58.675 --> 00:20:02.766
He didn't have to do
anything for me.

476
00:20:05.204 --> 00:20:07.162
He was my dad.

477
00:20:09.512 --> 00:20:11.775
Dad...

478
00:20:11.862 --> 00:20:14.213
you don't have to do
anything for me, either.

479
00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:17.999
But if you want to...

480
00:20:18.086 --> 00:20:19.392
that'd be great.

481
00:20:28.401 --> 00:20:30.272
I lost my dad.

482
00:20:32.143 --> 00:20:33.232
I know.

483
00:20:47.681 --> 00:20:48.769
Hello?

484
00:20:48.856 --> 00:20:50.161
I'm George Takei.

485
00:20:50.249 --> 00:20:52.338
I played Sulu on Star Trek.

486
00:20:52.425 --> 00:20:55.384
Did your grandfather
die recently?

487
00:20:55.471 --> 00:20:56.864
Yes.

488
00:20:56.951 --> 00:20:59.606
Allow me to extend
my deepest condolences.

489
00:20:59.693 --> 00:21:01.390
I got a call
from a neighbor

490
00:21:01.477 --> 00:21:03.523
that your father might
like to talk to me.

491
00:21:03.610 --> 00:21:06.003
And when I called back,
the line was busy.

492
00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:07.831
So I decided to come over.

493
00:21:07.918 --> 00:21:12.836
It was a six-hour bus ride
with a lot of stops.

494
00:21:12.923 --> 00:21:15.361
May I have some water, please?

495
00:21:15.448 --> 00:21:17.667
Sorry, my parents aren't home.

496
00:21:17.754 --> 00:21:19.321
But you can use
the hose out front.

497
00:21:19.408 --> 00:21:21.454
Delightful.
If you don't mind,

498
00:21:21.541 --> 00:21:22.977
I'll make camp
on your front lawn

499
00:21:23.064 --> 00:21:24.935
in anticipation
of their return.

500
00:21:25.022 --> 00:21:28.243
Improvisation:
my stock and trade.

501
00:21:28.330 --> 00:21:31.115
In episode 18,
"The Squire of Gothos,"

502
00:21:31.202 --> 00:21:35.772
the script read,
"the crew ad-libs 'hello.'"

503
00:21:35.859 --> 00:21:39.428
Well, I was off
to the races.

504
00:21:39.515 --> 00:21:41.996
Well, I've got
a lot of homework.

505
00:21:44.825 --> 00:21:47.088
Ooh, mushrooms!

506
00:21:47.175 --> 00:21:49.482
May I help myself?

507
00:22:27.911 --> 00:22:30.349
Captioned by Post Haste Digital